<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    The editor's design, made available to the editor's other assemblies. One `Import` of this file
    is the whole of what an editor assembly does to write `class="flex gap-2 bg-surface"` in a
    `.vxml` and have it mean something.

    ⚠ **This exists because "one project is one palette" was the right invariant with the wrong
    unit.** `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.targets` globs `**/vixen.ui.vcss` *within the consuming
    project*, so the only way an assembly could have tokens was to own a copy of them — and a second
    copy of the editor's palette is the precise failure the token model exists to prevent. The unit
    is the theme, and the editor is one theme across a dozen assemblies. See docs/plan/43 Part 3,
    shape C.

    ⚠ **What it was costing, measured rather than assumed.** Before this file, no assembly outside
    `Vixen.Editor.Ui` ran the step at all — the generation target is conditioned on there being a
    token source, so a project without one produced no sheet rather than a wrong one. Panels ported
    to VXML in `Vixen.Editor.Profiler` and `Vixen.Editor.AssetEditors` therefore fell back to
    tag-based rules, and the one utility class they do use — `hidden` — renders today only because
    `AdvancedTheme` hand-writes `.hidden { display: none; }` in `Vixen.Ui.Controls.Advanced`. The
    harm was never a wrong palette; it was a vocabulary that stopped at an assembly boundary and got
    paid for in hand-written duplicates.

    ⚠ **The sheets stay per-assembly, and that is the point of shape C rather than a compromise
    with it.** Each project scans only its own sources and emits only its own rules, so incremental
    build and project independence both survive; every sheet lands in `@layer utilities`, where
    document order does not decide anything, so the union of a dozen of them behaves at runtime as
    one sheet. What is shared is the token source, not the scan and not the output.

    A consuming project still has to load its own generated sheet into the document. The convention
    is that the assembly's existing `…Theme.Install(UiDocument)` does it, next to the hand-written
    sheet it already loads — see `AssetEditorTheme.Install`.
-->
<Project>

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
            ⚠ **A path and not a `ProjectReference` to read one out of, and the difference is
            evaluation order.** MSBuild cannot ask another project for a property during its own
            evaluation without building it, and the token source has to be an item before the
            generation target's `Inputs` are computed. So the editor's theme is named by location,
            and the `Error` in `Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.targets` is what stops that location from
            rotting quietly: a path that no longer resolves fails the build rather than reverting the
            assembly to the silence this file was written to end.
        -->
        <VixenStyleTokens Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../Theming/vixen.ui.vcss" />

        <!--
            ⚠ **Two canaries, and between them they say which palette this assembly is compiled
            against — which is the question that had no answer before.** A build step that silently
            does nothing is the failure this whole arrangement exists to remove, and "the sheet is
            not empty" is too weak a check to catch it: most of what the scanner finds are bare
            English words out of prose, so an assembly wired to the wrong tokens still emits `.flex`
            and `.hidden` and looks fine.

            `bg-surface` resolves only if the editor's `@theme` was read: the `color-surface` token
            is declared there and nowhere else. `bg-blue-500` resolves only if it was *not* — the
            editor's theme opens by setting the whole colour namespace to `initial`, emptying
            Tailwind's shipped palette, so a `.bg-blue-500` in one of these sheets means the project
            fell back to `ThemeTokens.CreateDefault()`. One must be present and the other absent, and
            `SharedThemeTests` in `Vixen.Editor.App.Tests` asserts both for every participant — that
            suite is also where the sabotage procedure for this file is written down.

            Safelisted rather than left to whatever the sources happen to say, because a canary that
            depends on a panel continuing to use a particular class is a canary that dies of a
            refactor. `bg-blue-500` costs nothing: under the editor's tokens it emits no rule at all,
            which is the assertion.
        -->
        <VixenStyleSafelist Include="bg-surface" />
        <VixenStyleSafelist Include="bg-blue-500" />

        <!--
            ⚠ This file declares arguments rather than reading files, so it has to be one of the
            timestamps the generation target watches or an edit here leaves every consumer's sheet
            stale and apparently up to date. That is not hypothetical: the two canaries above did
            exactly that on the build they were added.
        -->
        <VixenStyleBuildLogic Include="$(MSBuildThisFileFullPath)" />

        <!--
            ⚠ Named here rather than left to each consumer, because it is the same reason
            `Vixen.Editor.Ui` names it: MSBuild assets and build steps do not travel through a
            `ProjectReference`, so the tool that the imported `.targets` invokes has to be built
            first and only a `ProjectReference` says so. `ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"` keeps the
            tool out of every consumer's output.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../Tools/Vixen.StyleGen/Vixen.StyleGen.csproj"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                          PrivateAssets="all" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Import Project="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../../Core/Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities/build/Vixen.Ui.Styling.Utilities.targets" />

</Project>
